Proof. We will prove the claimed distance by using two properties of $C_{H}$ :


\begin{equation*}
\min _{\mathbf{c} \in C_{H}, \mathbf{c} \neq \mathbf{0}} w t(\mathbf{c})=3, \tag{1.10}
\end{equation*}


and


\begin{equation*}
\min _{\mathbf{c} \in C_{H}, \mathbf{c} \neq \mathbf{0}} w t(\mathbf{c})=\min _{\mathbf{c}_{1} \neq \mathbf{c}_{2} \in C_{H}} \Delta\left(\mathbf{c}_{1}, \mathbf{c}_{2}\right) \tag{1.11}
\end{equation*}


The proof of (1.10) follows from a case analysis on the Hamming weight of the message bits. Let us use $\mathbf{x}=\left(x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}, x_{4}\right)$ to denote the message vector.

\begin{itemize}
  \item[-] Case 0: If $w t(\mathbf{x})=0$, then $C_{H}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{0}$, which means we do not have to consider this codeword.
  \item[-] Case 1: If $w t(\mathbf{x})=1$ then at least two parity check bits in $\left(x_{2} \oplus x_{3} \oplus x_{4}, x_{1} \oplus x_{3} \oplus x_{4}, x_{1} \oplus x_{2} \oplus x_{4}\right)$ are 1 (see Exercise 1.10). So in this case, $w t\left(C_{H}(\mathbf{x})\right) \geq 3$.
  \item[-] Case 2: If $w t(\mathbf{x})=2$ then at least one parity check bit in $\left(x_{2} \oplus x_{3} \oplus x_{4}, x_{1} \oplus x_{3} \oplus x_{4}, x_{1} \oplus x_{2} \oplus x_{4}\right)$ is 1 (see Exercise 1.11). So in this case, $w t\left(C_{H}(\mathbf{x})\right) \geq 3$.
  \item[-] Case 3: If $w t(\mathbf{x}) \geq 3$ then those message bits themselves imply that $w t\left(C_{H}(\mathbf{x})\right) \geq 3$.
\end{itemize}

Thus, we can conclude that $\min _{\mathbf{c} \in C_{H}, \mathbf{c} \neq \mathbf{0}} w t(\mathbf{c}) \geq 3$. Further, note that $w t\left(C_{H}(1,0,0,0)\right)=3$, which implies that $\min _{\mathbf{c} \in C_{H}, \mathbf{c} \neq \mathbf{0}} w t(\mathbf{c}) \leq 3$. This along with the lower bound that we just obtained proves (1.10).

We now turn to the proof of (1.11). For the rest of the proof, let $\mathbf{x}=\left(x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}, x_{4}\right)$ and $\mathbf{y}=$ $\left(y_{1}, y_{2}, y_{3}, y_{4}\right)$ denote the two distinct messages. Using associativity and commutativity of the ⊕ operator, we obtain that

$$
C_{H}(\mathbf{x})+C_{H}(\mathbf{y})=C_{H}(\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y}),
$$

where the "+" operator is just the bit-wise ⊕ of the operand vectors ${ }^{11}$. Further, it can be verified that for two vectors $\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v} \in\{0,1\}^{n}$, we have:

$$
\Delta(\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v})=w t(\mathbf{u}+\mathbf{v})
$$

(see Exercise 1.12). Thus, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}
\min _{\mathbf{x} \neq \mathbf{y} \in\{0,1\}^{4}} \Delta\left(C_{H}(\mathbf{x}), C_{H}(\mathbf{y})\right) & =\min _{\mathbf{x} \neq \mathbf{y} \in\{0,1\}^{4}} w t\left(C_{H}(\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y})\right) \\
& =\min _{\mathbf{x} \neq \mathbf{0} \in\{0,1\}^{4}} w t\left(C_{H}(\mathbf{x})\right),
\end{aligned}
$$

where the second equality follows from the observation that $\left\{\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y} \mid \mathbf{x} \neq \mathbf{y} \in\{0,1\}^{n}\right\}=\left\{\mathbf{x} \in\{0,1\}^{n} \mid \mathbf{x} \neq\right.$ $\mathbf{0}\}$. Recall that $w t\left(C_{H}(\mathbf{x})\right)=0$ if and only if $\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{0}$ and this completes the proof of (1.11). Combining (1.10) and (1.11), we conclude that $C_{H}$ has a distance of 3. \(\square\)

