Proof. The proof is a straightforward generalization of the proof of Theorem 1.6.2. For notational convenience, let $e=\left\lfloor\frac{d-1}{2}\right\rfloor$. Given any two codewords, $\mathbf{c}_{1} \neq \mathbf{c}_{2} \in C$, the following is true (as $C$ has distance ${ }^{16} d$ ):


\begin{equation*}
B\left(\mathbf{c}_{1}, e\right) \cap B\left(\mathbf{c}_{2}, e\right)=\varnothing . \tag{1.17}
\end{equation*}


We claim that for all $\mathbf{x} \in[q]^{n}$,


\begin{equation*}
|B(\mathbf{x}, e)|=\sum_{i=0}^{e}\binom{n}{i}(q-1)^{i} . \tag{1.18}
\end{equation*}


Indeed any vector in $B(\mathbf{x}, e)$ must differ from $\mathbf{x}$ in exactly $0 \leq i \leq e$ positions. In the summation, $\binom{n}{i}$ is the number of ways of choosing the differing $i$ positions and in each such position, a vector can differ from $\mathbf{x}$ in $q-1$ ways.

Now consider the union of all Hamming balls centered around a codeword. Obviously, their union is a subset of $[q]^{n}$. In other words,


\begin{equation*}
\left|\bigcup_{\mathbf{c} \in C} B(\mathbf{c}, e)\right| \leq q^{n} . \tag{1.19}
\end{equation*}


As (1.17) holds for every pair of distinct codewords,


\begin{align*}
\left|\bigcup_{\mathbf{c} \in C} B(\mathbf{c}, e)\right| & =\sum_{\mathbf{c} \in C}|B(\mathbf{c}, e)| \\
& =q^{k} \sum_{i=0}^{e}\binom{n}{i}(q-1)^{i}, \tag{1.20}
\end{align*}


where (1.20) follows from (1.18) and the fact that $C$ has dimension $k$. Combining (1.20) and (1.19) and taking $\log _{q}$ of both sides we will get the desired bound:

$$
k \leq n-\log _{q}\left(\sum_{i=0}^{e}\binom{n}{i}(q-1)^{i}\right) .
$$ \(\square\)

Given any two codewords, $\mathbf{c}_{1} \neq \mathbf{c}_{2} \in C$, the following is true (as $C$ has distance ${ }^{16} d$ ):


\begin{equation*}
B\left(\mathbf{c}_{1}, e\right) \cap B\left(\mathbf{c}_{2}, e\right)=\varnothing . \tag{1.17}
\end{equation*}

We claim that for all $\mathbf{x} \in[q]^{n}$,


\begin{equation*}
|B(\mathbf{x}, e)|=\sum_{i=0}^{e}\binom{n}{i}(q-1)^{i} . \tag{1.18}
\end{equation*}


Indeed any vector in $B(\mathbf{x}, e)$ must differ from $\mathbf{x}$ in exactly $0 \leq i \leq e$ positions. In the summation, $\binom{n}{i}$ is the number of ways of choosing the differing $i$ positions and in each such position, a vector can differ from $\mathbf{x}$ in $q-1$ ways.

Now consider the union of all Hamming balls centered around a codeword. Obviously, their union is a subset of $[q]^{n}$. In other words,


\begin{equation*}
\left|\bigcup_{\mathbf{c} \in C} B(\mathbf{c}, e)\right| \leq q^{n} . \tag{1.19}
\end{equation*}

As (1.17) holds for every pair of distinct codewords,


\begin{align*}
\left|\bigcup_{\mathbf{c} \in C} B(\mathbf{c}, e)\right| & =\sum_{\mathbf{c} \in C}|B(\mathbf{c}, e)| \\
& =q^{k} \sum_{i=0}^{e}\binom{n}{i}(q-1)^{i}, \tag{1.20}
\end{align*}


where (1.20) follows from (1.18) and the fact that $C$ has dimension $k$.

